  "response": "Rival accounts of a practice's origin are adjudicated not by weighing plausibility in the abstract but by identifying the differing empirical commitments each account incurs. The hypothesis that a practice spread from a neighboring society is called diffusion, whereas the hypothesis of independent local invention is also termed independent innovation. The two accounts make different demands on the record, and the work of adjudication consists in locating the places where those demands can actually be checked.\n\nDemonstrating diffusion requires evidence of contact between the two societies. The fact that imported trade goods recovered at settlement sites document contact between two regions shows that this is the demand a diffusionist account must meet, and the same demand is confirmed by the circumstance that craft specialists migrated between the highland and lowland regions. At least one of that specialist movement and an exchange of raw materials between the highland and lowland regions along the river corridor obtained, and perhaps both obtained together.\n\nLexical evidence, by contrast, turns on phonological regularity rather than on the movement of goods. Regular sound correspondences allow linguists to separate borrowed terms from inherited ones. Comparative work on the languages of the two regions now fills several volumes; borrowed vocabulary is indistinguishable from inherited vocabulary by linguistic methods.\n\nChronology supplies a further line of attack, and its handling demands care. Radiocarbon measurements require calibration against a tree-ring curve to yield calendar ages. The laboratories serving both regions process several hundred samples a year; uncalibrated radiocarbon measurements are reported directly as calendar years. Once the calibration requirement is honored, the dated series indicates that the earliest securely dated example of the practice comes from the highland region; and in the nature of the case the earliest securely dated example lies either in the highland region or in the lowland region, one or the other and not both.\n\nA final difficulty is statistical, and it appears as soon as the question is posed across a sample of many societies. Galton's problem refers to the statistical non-independence of cultures linked by shared descent or contact, and recognition of that difficulty led to phylogenetic comparative methods, which test whether trait distributions track descent or contact networks. Adjudication therefore rests on convergence: contact evidence, loanword phonology, calibrated chronology and comparative statistics each constrain the problem from a different direction, and a verdict for diffusion or for independent innovation carries weight only when these constraints point the same way.",
